{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "פרי עץ הדר כפת תמרים", :translit "pri etz hadar kafat tmarim", :gloss "fruit of a beautiful tree, branches of palm trees"} {:he "פרי עץ הדר", :translit "pri etz hadar", :gloss "fruit of a beautiful tree"} {:he "אתרוגים", :translit "etrogim", :gloss "etrogs"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "פרי עץ הדר", :b "אתרוגים"}], :frame {:text "The verse commands taking 'the fruit of a beautiful tree' (Leviticus 23:40). The tradition identifies this fruit as the etrog based on the word 'hadar' ('beautiful') and its appearance, as discussed in Sukkah 35a where Rabbi Abahu derives the identification from the linguistic root meaning 'dwells' (ha-dar), since the etrog remains on the tree from year to year.", :sources ["Leviticus 23:40" "Sukkah 35a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "עבת", :translit "avat", :gloss "interwoven"} {:he "יבש", :translit "yaveish", :gloss "dried"} {:he "גזול", :translit "gazul", :gloss "stolen"} {:he "אשירה", :translit "asherah", :gloss "asherah"} {:he "עיר הנדחת", :translit "ir hanidachat", :gloss "city led astray"} {:he "נקטם ראשו", :translit "niktam rosho", :gloss "its tip is broken off"} {:he "ושל ע\"ג", :translit "ve'shel akum", :gloss "and that of idol worship"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Defective spelling of עבות (missing vav) signals six disqualifications of the lulav."}], :frame {:text "The lulav is invalid if its tip (the tif'eret, the central unopened leaf) is broken off, as taught in Sukkah 32a. A dried-out lulav is also invalid, since the verse calls the palm 'kappot tamarim,' read by the Sages as 'kappus tamarim' (bound of palm), implying freshness (Sukkah 32a). Use of stolen property for a mitzvah is broadly invalid based on the principle in Sukkah 30a, 'We have acquired it by a mitzvah,' applied specifically to the four species at Sukkah 29b–30a. If the lulav was worshipped as an idolatrous object it is invalid (Sukkah 29b). Deriving benefit from an asherah or from property of an ir hanidachat is prohibited by Deuteronomy 13:18 and the Sages extend this to disqualify objects used for mitzvot.", :sources ["Sukkah 29b" "Sukkah 30a" "Sukkah 32a" "Deuteronomy 13:18"]}} {:quotes [{:he "כפות תמרים", :translit "kapot tmarim", :gloss "branches of palm trees"} {:he "לולב", :translit "lulav", :gloss "lulav"} {:he "חיים", :translit "chayim", :gloss "life"} {:he "יבש", :translit "yaveish", :gloss "dried"} {:he "פסול", :translit "pasul", :gloss "disqualified"} {:he "עץ חיים", :translit "etz chayim", :gloss "tree of life"} {:he "תודיעני אורח חיים", :translit "todieni orach chayim", :gloss "make known to me the path of life"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "לולב", :b "חיים"} {:kind :gematria, :a "לולב", :b "חיים"}], :frame {:text "The Sages read 'kappot tamarim' (Leviticus 23:40) as referring to a single bound lulav (Sukkah 32a). A dried lulav is invalid (Sukkah 29b). The lulav is called 'עץ חיים' by analogy to Proverbs 3:18, where the Torah is called 'a tree of life to those who grasp it.' The phrase 'תודיעני אורח חיים' appears in Psalms 16:11.", :sources ["Leviticus 23:40" "Sukkah 29b" "Sukkah 32a" "Proverbs 3:18" "Psalms 16:11"]}}], :arithmetic [{:a-value 659, :b-value 660, :lens :standard, :equal? false, :kind :gematria, :b "אתרוגים", :by :selah, :colel? true, :a "פרי עץ הדר"} {:kind :other, :note "Defective spelling of עבות (missing vav) signals six disqualifications of the lulav.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :gematria, :a "לולב", :a-value 68, :b "חיים", :b-value 68, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true} {:kind :gematria, :a "לולב", :a-value 68, :b "חיים", :b-value 68, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:25:21.713854214Z", :model "glm-5.2"}